--------------------------------
await with Playwright Assertions vs Methods
--------------------------------

The difference is because toBeChecked() is a Playwright assertion, while isChecked() is a method that returns a value.

1. await outside

await expect(checkBox).not.toBeChecked();

Here, expect(checkBox).not.toBeChecked() is a Playwright assertion.
Playwright needs to wait and retry the assertion until it passes or the assertion timeout is reached.

So:

await
 ↓
Wait for the Playwright assertion to complete

2. await inside

expect(await radioBtns.last().isChecked()).toBeTruthy();

Here, isChecked() is a Playwright method that returns a value (true or false).
So we first need to wait for isChecked() to give us the result:

await radioBtns.last().isChecked()
             ↓
          true/false
             ↓
expect(...)
             ↓
toBeTruthy()

That's why await is inside expect().

Easy way to remember

Playwright Assertion
await expect(locator).toBeChecked();

Get a value first
expect(await locator.isChecked()).toBeTruthy();

Key difference:

toBeChecked() → assertion → await goes outside
isChecked() → returns a value → await goes inside

Yes. toBeChecked() is also a method, but more specifically, it is an assertion matcher provided by Playwright Test.

The important difference is what the method does and what it returns:
await expect(checkBox).toBeChecked();

toBeChecked() → assertion matcher
It verifies that the checkbox is checked.
Playwright automatically waits/retries the assertion.
Therefore, await is placed outside the complete assertion.

Whereas:
expect(await checkBox.isChecked()).toBeTruthy();

isChecked() → Playwright locator method
It returns a value: true or false.
We need await to get that value first.
Then expect() verifies the returned value.

Simple comparison

toBeChecked()
    → Verify directly
    → Playwright assertion
    → await outside

isChecked()
    → Get true/false
    → Playwright method
    → await inside
	
	
So your earlier understanding can be refined to:

Both are methods, but toBeChecked() is an assertion matcher, 
while isChecked() is a locator method that returns a boolean.

--------------------------------
Handling Child Windows / New Tabs Using context.waitForEvent('page')
--------------------------------

This code handles a new browser tab/window (child page) that opens when clicking a link.


test.only('Child Window Playwright test', async ({page, context}) => {  

    await page.goto("https://rahulshettyacademy.com/loginpagePractise/");

    const documentLink = page.locator('.blinkingText');

    const [newPage] = await Promise.all([
        context.waitForEvent('page'),
        documentLink.first().click(),
    ]);

    const text = await newPage.locator('.red').textContent();
    console.log(text);
});


1. page and context

async ({page, context})

page → Current browser tab.
context → Browser context containing the current page and any new pages/tabs opened from it.

2. Locate the link

const documentLink = page.locator('.blinkingText');

→ Creates a locator for the link that opens the child window/tab.

3. Wait for the new page

const [newPage] = await Promise.all([
    context.waitForEvent('page'),
    documentLink.first().click(),
]);

This is the main part of the code.

context.waitForEvent('page')

→ Tells Playwright:
	"Wait for a new page/tab to be opened."
	
documentLink.first().click()

→ Clicks the link that opens the new page.

Why Promise.all()?

Both operations need to happen together:

Wait for new page
       +
Click the link
       ↓
New page opens
       ↓
newPage receives the new tab

newPage represents the newly opened child window/tab.

4. Get text from the child window

const text = await newPage.locator('.red').textContent();

Notice that we use:
	newPage
instead of:
	page

because .red is located on the newly opened page.

5. Print the text

console.log(text);
→ Prints the text from the child window in the console.

Easy Flow

Main Page
   ↓
Find Child Window Link
   ↓
Click Link
   ↓
New Tab/Window Opens
   ↓
Capture New Page
   ↓
newPage
   ↓
Locate Element
   ↓
Get Text
   ↓
Print Text

Key Point

When clicking an element opens a new tab/window, 
use context.waitForEvent('page') to wait for and capture the newly opened page.

--------------------------------
EXTRACTING EMAIL DOMAIN USING split() IN JAVASCRIPT/TYPESCRIPT
--------------------------------

Reference Text:

Please email us at mentor@rahulshettyacademy.com with below template to receive response


Code:

const arrayText = text.split('@');
const domain = arrayText[1].split(' ')[0];

console.log(domain);


What happens overall?

Complete text
     ↓
split('@')
     ↓
Separate text at @
     ↓
Take the text after @
     ↓
split(' ')
     ↓
Take the first part
     ↓
rahulshettyacademy.com


--------------------------------
1. FIRST LINE
--------------------------------

const arrayText = text.split('@');


split('@')

→ Splits the complete text wherever `@` occurs.

Text:

Please email us at mentor@rahulshettyacademy.com with below template to receive response


After split('@'):

arrayText[0]
→ Please email us at mentor

arrayText[1]
→ rahulshettyacademy.com with below template to receive response


So, the required domain is now inside:

arrayText[1]


--------------------------------
2. SECOND LINE
--------------------------------

const domain = arrayText[1].split(' ')[0];


First:

arrayText[1]

contains:

rahulshettyacademy.com with below template to receive response


Then:

.split(' ')

splits the text wherever there is a space.

Result:

[0] → rahulshettyacademy.com
[1] → with
[2] → below
[3] → template
[4] → to
[5] → receive
[6] → response


Then:

[0]

selects the first item:

rahulshettyacademy.com


Therefore:

domain = "rahulshettyacademy.com"


--------------------------------
3. PRINT THE DOMAIN
--------------------------------

console.log(domain);


→ Prints the extracted domain in the console.


Output:

rahulshettyacademy.com


--------------------------------
IMPORTANT CONCEPT
--------------------------------

This code uses TWO split() operations:

text.split('@')
    ↓
Separates the text at @

arrayText[1].split(' ')
    ↓
Separates the remaining text at the first space

[0]
    ↓
Takes the first part


Final:

mentor@rahulshettyacademy.com
        ↓
rahulshettyacademy.com


NOTE:

This approach works for this particular sentence format.

It is a simple string-manipulation technique and is not a
general-purpose email parser.

