------------------------------------
DIFFERENCE BETWEEN textContent() AND inputValue() IN PLAYWRIGHT
------------------------------------

The main difference is what type of data they read from an element.


------------------------------------
1. textContent()
------------------------------------

Used to get the text content inside an HTML element.

Example:

<h4>iPhone X</h4>

const text = await page.locator('h4').textContent();

console.log(text);


Output:

iPhone X


So:

textContent()
→ Gets the text content of an element.


------------------------------------
2. inputValue()
------------------------------------

Used to get the current value of an input-type element.

Example:

<input id="username" value="rahul">

const value = await page.locator('#username').inputValue();

console.log(value);


Output:

rahul


So:

inputValue()
→ Gets the current value of an <input>, <textarea>, or <select> element.


------------------------------------
EASY COMPARISON
------------------------------------

| Method          | Used For                         | Example                 |
|-----------------|----------------------------------|-------------------------|
| textContent()   | Text content of an element      | <h4>iPhone X</h4>       |
| inputValue()    | Current value of form element   | <input value="rahul">   |


------------------------------------
REMEMBER THIS
------------------------------------

Normal text element
        ↓
textContent()


Input / Textarea / Select
        ↓
inputValue()


------------------------------------
EXAMPLE FROM PREVIOUS CODE
------------------------------------

await page.locator('#username').fill(domain);


To verify what was entered into the username field:

console.log(await page.locator('#username').inputValue());


Output:

rahulshettyacademy.com


------------------------------------
SIMPLE RULE
------------------------------------

textContent()
→ Reads text content from an element.

inputValue()
→ Reads the current value of a form element such as
  <input>, <textarea>, or <select>.